'''Nahuel Huapi Lake''' ({{langx|es|Lago Nahuel Huapi}}) is an [[Andes|Andean]] [[lake]] in the lake region of northern [[Patagonia]] between the provinces of [[Río Negro Province|Río Negro]] and [[Neuquén Province|Neuquén]], in [[Argentina]]. The lake has a northwest-southeast elongated shape and complex geography with several branches, peninsulas and islands. The city of [[Bariloche]] is on the southern shore of the lake and the town of [[Villa La Angostura]] lies on its northwestern shores. The lake is wholly inside [[Nahuel Huapi National Park]]. It is one of the largest lakes in northern Patagonia. It is drained by [[Limay River]] and it is part of the watershed of [[Río Negro (Argentina)|Negro River]] which discharges into the [[South Atlantic]]. The lake is, Argentinas deepest non-[[cryptodepression]] (the deepest point is well over mean sea level).

==Etymology==
The name of the lake derives from the toponym of its major island in [[Mapudungun]] ([[Mapuche]] language): "Island of [[Puma (genus)|Puma]]", from ''nahuel'', 'puma', and ''huapí'', 'island'. There is, however, more to the word "Nahuel"—it can also signify 'a man who by [[Magic (paranormal)|sorcery]] has been [[Shapeshifting|transformed]] to a puma'.

==Geography==
[[Image:Nahuel Huapi Lake, Llao Llao.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Lake Nahuel Huapi. The surrounding area became Argentina's first National Park in 1903]]
[[File:Lake Nahuel Huapi, Argentina (NASA, 1997).jpg|thumb|250px|Lake Nahuel Huapi from space (the elongated, dark feature in the center of the image is the lake and in the bottom is seen the Limay River), North is to the right of the image, 1997.]]

Nahuel Huapi lake, located within the [[Nahuel Huapi National Park]], has a surface of {{convert|530|km2|abbr=on}}, rests {{convert|2510|ft|order=flip}} above the sea level, and has a maximum measured depth (as of 2007) of {{convert|1522|ft|order=flip}}.

Its seven branches are named ''Blest'' ({{convert|36|km2|abbr=on|disp=or}}), ''Huemul''  ({{convert|21.5|km2|abbr=on|disp=or}}), ''de la Tristeza'' ({{convert|18.5|km2|abbr=on|disp=or}}), ''Campanario'' ({{convert|7.9|km2|abbr=on|disp=or}}), ''Machete'', ''del Rincón'' and ''Última Esperanza''. It is connected to other smaller lakes such as ''[[Gutiérrez Lake|Gutiérrez]]'', ''Moreno'', ''[[Lago Espejo|Espejo]]'' and ''Correntoso''. The deep-blue waters hold a number of islands, most notably {{ill|Victoria Island (Argentina)|lt=Isla Victoria|es|Isla Victoria (Argentina)}} with an area of {{convert|31|km2|abbr=on}}, and [[Huemul Island|''Isla Huemul'']] on the south end of the lake.

The lake's crystal clear waters are very susceptible to climate changes and have an average surface temperature of {{convert|45|F|C|order=flip}}; this makes it both beautiful and treacherous. [[Hypothermia]] is one of the risks bathers must undertake. [[Kayaking]] is a popular sport on this and adjacent lakes.
The lake is also the starting point of the [[Limay River]].

==Limnology and geology==
The lake depression consists of several [[glacial valley]]s carved out along [[fault (geology)|fault]]s and [[Miocene]] valleys that were later [[Moraine-dammed lake|dammed by moraines]]. During the [[Last Glacial Maximum]] of the [[Llanquihue glaciation]] the lake basin was wholly occupied by a glacier.<ref name=Heusser2004-25-29>{{cite book |last=Heusser |first=C.J. |date=2004 |title=Ice Age Southern Andes |publisher=Elsevier |pages=25–29 }}</ref>

The lake has usually a blue colour but on occasion its water have turned [[turquoise]] or green after earthquakes in southern Chile such as the one [[1960 Valdivia earthquake|on May 22, 1960]] and the one [[List of earthquakes in Chile|on January 2, 2011]].<ref name=K1963/><ref>{{Cite news |title=Investigan cambio de color en aguas del Nahuel Huapi |url=https://www.rionegro.com.ar/investigan-cambio-de-color-en-aguas-del-nahuel-FXRN_533622/ |date=2011-01-05 |access-date=2023-03-14 |work=Río Negro |language=Spanish}}</ref><ref name="Araucania2011USGS">{{cite web|url=https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eqinthenews/2011/usc0000y49/ |title=Magnitude 7.1 – ARAUCANIA, CHILE |publisher=Earthquake.usgs.gov |access-date=2012-04-12 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110105111847/http://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eqinthenews/2011/usc0000y49/ |archive-date=2011-01-05 }}</ref>

The June [[2011 Puyehue eruption|2011 eruption]] of the [[Puyehue-Cordón Caulle]] volcanic complex, in neighboring Chile, caused parts of the lake's surface to be blanketed in [[volcanic ash]].<ref>{{Cite news| title=Chilean volcano fills lake with ash|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/earth/earthvideo/8580835/Chilean-volcano-fills-lake-with-ash.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618005235/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/earth/earthvideo/8580835/Chilean-volcano-fills-lake-with-ash.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=18 June 2011|date=16 June 2011 | location=London|work=The Daily Telegraph}}</ref>

The dominant species of [[plankton]] in the winter half-year in the lake is ''[[Dictyosphaerium pulchellum]]''.<ref name=K1963>{{Cite book |title=Araucanian Lakes: Plankton Studies in North Patagonia with Notes on Terrestrial Vegetation |last=Thomasson |first=Kuno |publisher=[[Almqvist & Wiksell]] |year=1963 |location=[[Uppsala]] |page=80-84 |chapter=Lake Nahuel Huapi |authorlink=Kuno Thomasson}}</ref> The remainder of the year ''[[Bacillariophyceae]]'' and ''[[Chrysophyceae]]'' algae dominate.<ref name=K1963/>

==Fauna==
This lake harbors several [[Introduced species|introduced]], non-native species of trout,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bariloche.org/paginas/2007/02/27/la_fauna_del_parque_nacional_nahuel_huapi/ |title=La Fauna del Parque Nacional Nahuel Huapi |publisher=Bariloche.Org |access-date=2013-01-02}}</ref> including [[rainbow trout]], [[brown trout]] and [[brook trout]] which attract [[Fisherman|anglers]] from the world over.

A curious fact about the lake is that, despite being nowhere near any ocean and being at high altitude, it is also home for [[kelp gull]] and the [[Cormorant|blue eyed cormorant]] (Phalacrocorax atriceps), otherwise strictly marine birds.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.birdsoman.com/Birds/017-Cormorants/BlueeyedCormorant/BlueeyedCormorant.htm |title=Blue-eyed Cormorant |publisher=Birdsoman.com |access-date=2013-01-02}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ripioturismo.com.ar/np-nh.htm |title=NAHUEL HUAPI NATIONAL PARK - ARGENTINA - National Parks in Argentina - Ripio Incoming Tour Operator Argentina |publisher=Ripioturismo.com.ar |date=1903-11-06 |access-date=2013-01-02}}</ref><ref>[https://www.jstor.org/stable/1368895 Pamela C. Rasmussen ''The Condor'' Vol. 88, No. 3 (Aug., 1986), pp. 393-395. University of California Press]</ref>

==Culture==
===''Nahuelito''===
{{main|Nahuelito}}

At the beginning of the 20th century, and following an old aboriginal legend,<ref>{{cite web|author1=Sam Mustafa|title=The Myth of Nahuelito: A Monstrous Symbol of Argentina|url=http://www.argentinaindependent.com/life-style/travel-life-style/the-myth-of-nahuelito-a-monstrous-symbol-of-argentina/|website=Argentina Independent|access-date=3 March 2017|language=EN|date=25 November 2010|archive-date=4 March 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170304114453/http://www.argentinaindependent.com/life-style/travel-life-style/the-myth-of-nahuelito-a-monstrous-symbol-of-argentina/|url-status=dead}}</ref> the rumor of a giant creature living in the deep waters of the lake took up. The ''creature'' is known locally as ''Nahuelito''. Reported sightings of it predate [[Nessie]] and [[Arthur Conan Doyle]]'s ''[[The Lost World (Doyle novel)|The Lost World]]'' (1912).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.strangemag.com/nahuelito.html |title=Lake Monsters: Nahuelito |publisher=Strangemag.com |access-date=2013-01-02}}</ref>

Local [[Mapuche]] called another creature ''el Cuero'' (leather) for its smooth skin.{{citation needed|date=February 2021}}  The neighboring lake [[Lago Lácar]], has also been the site for accounts of another creature, more consistent with a [[plesiosaur]], with aborigines describing it as a ''sea-cow'' with ''teeth'' all around it.{{citation needed|date=February 2021}}

Members of the [[Buenos Aires Zoo]] visited the lake in 1922 trying to corroborate the reports of sightings of the prehistoric animal, but found no evidence to support the theory of such a creature.

===Hitler conspiracy theory===
[[File:Casa Inalco.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|The Inalco House near the current settlement of [[Villa La Angostura]]. According to the fringe theory, Hitler lived some years here after 1945.<ref name=ElTiempo/>]]
{{See also|Conspiracy theories about Adolf Hitler's death}}

Some works, such as the ''[[National Police Gazette]]'' (circa 1950–1970), an American [[Tabloid journalism|tabloid-style]] magazine,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Westlake |first=Steven A. |url=https://archive.org/details/hitlerisalive0000unse/page/176/ |title=Hitler Is Alive! |publisher=[[Mysterious Press]] |year=2016 |isbn=978-1-5040-2215-6 |location=New York |pages=176, 216-17, 221-22, 394-95, 399, 402 |language=en}}</ref> as well as a 2004 book by Abel Basti and the 2011 book ''[[Grey Wolf: The Escape of Adolf Hitler|Grey Wolf]]'' claim that [[Adolf Hitler]] and [[Eva Braun]] did not [[Death of Adolf Hitler|commit suicide]] but escaped to Argentina [[Ratlines (World War II)|along with other Nazis]] and lived in the surroundings of [[Bariloche]] for many years after World War&nbsp;II. Hitler and Braun allegedly resided at the '''Inalco House''', located at the northwest end of the lake,<ref name=ElTiempo>{{Cite news|title=NAZI-GUÍA TURÍSTICA POR BARILOCHE|url=https://www.eltiempo.com/archivo/documento/MAM-1584356|date=January 2, 2004|work=[[El Tiempo (Colombia)|El Tiempo]]|access-date=August 7, 2019|language=es}}</ref> in part due to the estate's remoteness and lack of accessibility.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Diaz |first=Jesus |date=2011-11-16 |title=Hitler's Secret Argentine Sanctuary Is for Sale, Say Conspiracy Theorists |url=https://gizmodo.com/hitlers-secret-argentine-sanctuary-is-for-sale-say-con-5860250 |access-date=2022-12-30 |website=Gizmodo |language=en}}</ref>

The son of real-estate businessman [[Primo Capraro]] sold the property to architect [[Alejandro Bustillo]], who designed the house in early 1943. The plan includes unusual design features such as bedrooms connected by bathrooms (and closets), similar to Hitler's [[Berghof (residence)|Berghof]] residence in Bavaria.<ref name=":1" /> Bustillo sold the estate to Enrique García Merou, a [[Buenos Aires]] lawyer linked to several German businesses; Merou is alleged to have assisted the [[Ratlines (World War II)|Nazi ratlines]]. The residence was later sold to businessman [[Jorge Antonio]], the representative of German automaker [[Mercedes-Benz]] in Argentina and a close associate of Argentine president [[Juan Perón]].<ref name=":1" /> In 1970, the house was sold to José Rafael Trozzo, who also bought properties owned by escaped ''[[Schutzstaffel]]'' (SS) officer [[Reinhard Kopps]], who—along with Capraro—had ties to SS commander [[Erich Priebke]]. The Trozzo family put the house up for sale in 2011.<ref name=":1" /> 

According to the fringe theory, a number of [[U-boat]]s ferried certain high level Nazis including Adolf Hitler and Eva Braun and a large amount of [[Nazi loot]] to Argentina, where the Nazis were given refuge by Perón, who, with his wife [[Eva Perón|Evita]], had been receiving money from the Nazis for some time. Hitler allegedly went to Argentina, first staying at Hacienda San Ramón, a large ranch east of [[San Carlos de Bariloche]].<ref name="ElTiempo" /> Hitler then moved to a Bavarian-style mansion at Inalco where he remained until Peron was overthrown in a [[Revolución Libertadora|military coup]] in 1955, when he fled to an even more remote [[chalet]] to avoid detection when the new government in Buenos Aires began looking for Nazi war criminals alleged to be hiding in Argentina. Purportedly, Eva Braun left Hitler around 1954 and moved to [[Neuquén]] with their daughter, Ursula ('Uschi'), and Hitler died in February 1962.<ref name="ElTiempo" /><ref name="fbi">{{cite web|url=http://vault.fbi.gov/adolf-hitler/adolf-hitler-part-01-of-04/view|title=FBI &mdash; Adolf Hitler Part 01 of 04 - File No 105-410 |publisher=vault.fbi.gov|access-date=3 September 2014}}</ref><ref name="TheGuardian">{{Cite news |last=Thorpe |first=Vanessa |date=October 27, 2013 |title=Hitler lived until 1962? That's my story, claims Argentinian writer |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/oct/27/hitler-lived-1962-argentina-plagiarism |access-date=August 7, 2019}}</ref><ref name="JapanTimes">{{Cite news|title=Hitler escape book's authors in plagiarism row|url=https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2013/10/27/world/hitler-escape-books-authors-in-plagiarism-row/#.XU6Ue-hKjIU|last=Thope|first=Vanessa|date=October 27, 2013|work=[[The Japan Times]]|access-date=August 8, 2019}}</ref>

Citing a former Nazi presence in Bariloche, the investigative series ''[[Hunting Hitler]]'' (2015–2018) reveals a guard tower—reportedly built by the same architect as the Inalco House—looking over the lake (situated closer to Bariloche than the house), as well as a destroyed bunker on the other side of the lake;<ref name=":0">{{Cite episode |title=Anatomy of a Manhunt |series=Hunting Hitler |network=[[History (American TV network)|History]] |date=2017 |season=3 |number=0 |minutes=19:45–22, 31}}</ref> together the two sites (in addition to other possible lookouts such as a wooden building resembling a guard shack)<ref>{{Cite episode |title=Hitler's Safehouse |series=Hunting Hitler |network=History |date=2015 |season=1 |number=6 |minutes=28:30–29}}</ref> may have provided a panoramic view used to safeguard the mansion,<ref name=":0" /> then accessible from only the lake due to heavy forestation and long rumoured to have housed Hitler.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=Inside the Investigation |series=Hunting Hitler |network=History |date=2016 |season=2 |number=0 |minutes=25}}</ref> Additionally, the ''Hunting Hitler'' team cited the proximity of German scientist [[Ronald Richter]]'s [[Huemul Project|Perón-backed nuclear fusion project]] on Huemul Island.<ref name=":0" />

In a 2018 episode of ''[[Expedition Unknown]]'', Abel Basti secured a rare excursion into the Inalco House, revealing little except for some old kitchen utensils in the basement. Using a metal detector on the grounds, host [[Josh Gates]] located a [[Reichsmark|Nazi coin]], leading him to conclude that Nazis (but not necessarily Hitler) could have used the house.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=Nazis in Argentina |series=Expedition Unknown |network=[[Discovery Channel]] |date=2018 |season=5 |number=3 |minutes=22–30, 35–37, 38:30–41}}</ref>

